merge(W + 'entities/joe-biden.md', """## Post-debate crisis (July 2024)

In the days after his June 27, 2024 debate performance, Biden faced an escalating crisis of confidence within his own party.[N] The White House scrambled to prevent his candidacy from being enveloped in a full-blown crisis, and the campaign launched a counter-offensive of furious networking among senior Democrats to counteract fears that the 81-year-old president's frail appearance had made defeat inevitable in November.[N] Several Democratic state governors publicly avowed loyalty while quietly positioning themselves as potential late substitutes, watching for further slippage in his fragile polling and donor support; [[gavin-newsom|Gavin Newsom]] said he would "never turn my back" on the president but was widely seen as ambiguous in declaring himself ready "to step up."[N] Reporting indicated that [[kamala-harris|Kamala Harris]] would be the clear front-runner in any open convention.[N]

Biden's own family privately blamed his campaign high command for the debate flop at a Camp David gathering that weekend, urging him to fire or demote senior staff including [[anita-dunn|Anita Dunn]], [[bob-bauer|Bob Bauer]], and [[ron-klain|Ron Klain]], with complaints that he had been unprepared to attack, too focused on defending his record, and overworked and under-rested; there was no immediate expectation he would act on the advice.[N] Badlands contributors framed the episode variously: [[ashe-in-america|Ashe in America]] described Biden as visibly demented and the election as effectively already decided, while [[burning-bright|Burning Bright]] argued through a [[devolution]] lens that Biden may be operating for patriots either directly, as a cooperating human source under a plea arrangement, or indirectly through game theory, and that the Old Guard's decision to stand pat reflected fear that they might be unable to replace him.[N]

Separately, House Republicans sued Attorney General [[merrick-garland|Merrick Garland]] on July 1, 2024 to obtain the audio of Biden's interview with the special counsel in his classified documents case, after the White House asserted executive privilege to withhold it from Congress.[N]
""")

merge(W + 'entities/donald-trump-jr.md', """## Middle East business travel (2024)

In late June 2024, Newsweek published an article titled "Donald Trump Jr.'s Trip to Middle East Raises Eyebrows," citing his social media posts announcing a 2028 opening date for the villa community and golf course planned near Muscat, Oman under the [[trump-international-oman|Trump International]] brand.[N] The project is a joint venture between [[trump-organization|The Trump Organization]] and [[dar-global|Dar Global]], the international arm of Saudi developer [[dar-al-arkan|Dar Al Arkan]].[N] Badlands contributor [[ghostofbasedpatrickhenry|GhostofBasedPatrickHenry]] argued that Gulf principals would deliberately keep dealings with Trump Jr., [[eric-trump|Eric Trump]], and [[jared-kushner|Jared Kushner]] low-key out of respect for [[donald-trump|Donald Trump]]'s presidential campaign.[N]
""")

merge(W + 'entities/benjamin-netanyahu.md', """## Haaretz assessment after the 2024 debate

Following the June 2024 [[2024-presidential-debate|presidential debate]], a Haaretz opinion column argued that Netanyahu had been waiting for a signal months ahead of the U.S. election as to whether it would pay him, after the failure of October 7, to keep clinging to power until [[joe-biden|Joe Biden]] was replaced — and that on debate night he received his answer.[N] The columnist contended that despite excitement among Netanyahu's supporters over Biden's collapse, a [[donald-trump|Trump]] presidency would be bad for the Israeli prime minister, because Trump "feeds on weakness" and does not like losers.[N] In that assessment Netanyahu qualified as easy prey: he had not destroyed Hamas, was sinking in the Gaza quagmire without a rescue plan, had rejected a regional arrangement that would have secured the return of the hostages, presided over a deteriorating economy, lacked public support, and faced a likely International Criminal Court arrest warrant.[N]

Badlands contributor [[ghostofbasedpatrickhenry|GhostofBasedPatrickHenry]] agreed that Trump's win looked inevitable and would unsettle Netanyahu, but rejected the "loser persona" explanation, arguing instead that the [[abraham-accords|Abraham Accords]] had been wielded as a political weapon to ignite a peaceful counterinsurgency in the region and inside Israel against Netanyahu and what he calls the Israeli Deep State.[N]
""")

merge(W + 'entities/volodymyr-zelensky.md', """## Wartime elite optics (July 2024)

In early July 2024, Russian outlet Mockba 24 reported that Zelensky's wife [[olena-zelenska|Olena Zelenska]] had purchased a Bugatti Tourbillon hypercar for €4.5 million, citing the French site Verite Cachee and a Paris Bugatti showroom employee, with delivery expected in 2026.[N] The report was paired in the Badlands brief with Guardian reporting on Ukrainian men in hiding or fleeing the country to evade conscription, amid a nationwide ban on men aged 18 to 60 leaving and sweeping new mobilisation measures adopted in 2024 that expanded call-ups and stiffened penalties for draft evasion.[N] Badlands contributor [[ghostofbasedpatrickhenry|GhostofBasedPatrickHenry]] presented the juxtaposition as evidence of elite excess funded by Western taxpayers against mass suffering, calling the whole arrangement "a scam."[N]
""")

merge(W + 'entities/gavin-newsom.md', """## Positioning after the 2024 debate

In the days after the June 2024 [[2024-presidential-debate|presidential debate]], Newsom — California governor and a key surrogate for [[joe-biden|Joe Biden]] — defended the president immediately after his poor performance, saying he would "never turn my back" on him.[N] He was nonetheless widely seen as expressing ambiguity by adding that he was ready "to step up," and was counted among several Democratic governors reported to be subtly positioning themselves as potential late substitutes while avoiding the appearance of doing so.[N] Badlands contributor [[ashe-in-america|Ashe in America]] noted the irony that even if Biden stepped aside and the nomination were opened at the August convention, [[kamala-harris|Kamala Harris]] would be the clear front-runner, leaving the ambitious governors out of turn.[N]
""")
